    "In 1739 a twenty-six-year-old Scot published the boldest book of the century. He had drafted most of it in France before he was twenty-six, in a country town where Descartes had gone to school a hundred years earlier, and he meant it to do for the human mind what Newton had done for the heavens: find the laws underneath. He titled it *A Treatise of Human Nature*, and into its three volumes he packed arguments that would still be unanswered three centuries later. Almost nobody read it. In his own famous, rueful phrase, written near the end of his life, it \"fell dead-born from the press.\"",
