          "p": "The rule that makes the whole machine run, the one Hume swings like a hammer for the rest of his career, is the **copy principle**: \"That all our simple ideas in their first appearance are deriv'd from simple impressions, which are correspondent to them, and which they exactly represent.\" In plain terms: every idea in the head is a copy of some impression actually had. Ideas can be shuffled and recombined freely (a golden mountain comes from gluing together gold and a mountain, both already seen), but a *simple* idea cannot be conjured out of nothing. A man born blind has no idea of red, because he never had the impression. This sounds like a modest point about psychology. It is in fact a weapon, and Hume sets out exactly how he means to use it: whenever a philosopher waves around some grand abstract term (*substance*, *necessary connection*, *the self*) that is suspected of being empty noise, put it to the test. *From what impression is that idea derived?* Trace the term back to the experience that supposedly stamped it on the mind. If the impression turns up, the idea is legitimate. If not, the term \"has no determinate meaning\": it is a word with nothing behind it, and out it goes. The copy principle is a meaning-detector, and most of philosophy and theology is about to fail the test."
